scanopy-backup: nightly pull of the Scanopy database to the monitoring VM

Why: the Scanopy server, Postgres and hypervisor-local daemon run on the 0docker hypervisor, which PBS does not back up (it backs
up guests). Losing that host would lose every daemon key, credential assignment, tag and schedule. The monitoring VM lives in another
fleet (0mcp), so a dump kept here survives the loss of 0docker.

Each night (03:20 UTC) the job runs `ssh root@example.net dump` with /etc/scanopy-backup/id_ed25519. The key is a forced command
(/usr/local/sbin/scanopy-backup-hv on the hypervisor) that can only stream `pg_dump | gzip`. The dump is written as .part, verified
(gzip integrity, size, pg_dump completion trailer, the core tables), moved to /var/backups/scanopy/scanopy-<UTC>.sql.gz (0600) and
the newest 14 are kept. A failed run leaves older dumps untouched.

Files
  /opt/scanopy-backup/scanopy_backup.py        the job (python3 stdlib)
  /opt/scanopy-backup/scanopy_backup_hv.sh     copy of the hypervisor wrapper (install as /usr/local/sbin/scanopy-backup-hv)
  /etc/scanopy-backup/id_ed25519(.pub)         dedicated key; known_hosts is kept next to it
  /var/backups/scanopy/                        the dumps (root only; they hold credential material)
  /var/lib/prometheus/node-exporter/scanopy_backup.prom   metrics scanopy_backup_*
  systemd: scanopy-backup.service (oneshot) + scanopy-backup.timer

Manual run:  sudo systemctl start scanopy-backup.service ; journalctl -u scanopy-backup -n 20
Dry run:     sudo python3 /opt/scanopy-backup/scanopy_backup.py --dry-run
Restore:     see docs/runbooks/scanopy-service-discovery.md, "Database backup".
Rollback:    systemctl disable --now scanopy-backup.timer
Managed by the monitoring_vm role (tasks/scanopy_backup.yml).
